{
  "dom_id": "step-q325862",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{9}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{6}+1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{6} & =0 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+4 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(-4,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095453/assets/150b21d1b8ddd6f837981cbd5a9771d3587ebe07b8f0aaa61183a0498a100a5d.png)",
